-- title:
--   Integrality over ℚ̄[j,j_N] gives regularity at affine places
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a positive level $N$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$, together with modular polynomial data `data` for $q$, a proof `hKr` that its bivariate reduction mod $q$ equals $(X^q - Y)(X - Y^q)$ in the project's normalisation, and proofs $h\alpha$, $h\beta$ that the two degeneracy maps $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-base-changed from level $N$ to level $Nq$ are integral. Let $P$ be a `PlaceSpecialization` for these data: it provides a map `P.sp` from places of $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldBar N` (the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full level-$N$ modular function field inside Laurent series) to places of the characteristic-$q$ modular function field `modularFunctionFieldC k N`, a homomorphism on degree-zero divisor classes, and compatibilities relating orders of vanishing of $j$ and of $j_N$ (and of their poles) at $w$ and at `P.sp w`. Let $g \in F$ be integral over the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subalgebra of $F$ generated by the coefficient-embedded $q$-expansions $j$ and $j_N = j(q^N)$, and let $U$ be a place of $F$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (a valuation subring containing $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, proper, and a principal ideal ring) such that `P.sp U` is an affine geometric place, i.e. both `jGeomGen k N` and `jNGeomGen k N` lie in its valuation subring. Then $g$ lies in the valuation subring of $U$.
--
--   This is the statement that the affine model of the modular curve in characteristic $q$ detects regularity upstairs: the specialised place being affine forces $j$ and $j_N$ to be regular at $U$, hence so is anything integral over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}[j,j_N]$. It is used in the construction of prolongation tuples for the Hecke correspondence and in the lifting statement for functions whose coefficients satisfy a $q$-power congruence.
